<a name="Intro"></a>
<h1>Introduction</h1>
This example shows how to observe the MPI communication that happens inside a distributed Ginkgo run.

Ginkgo's logging framework, normally used to instrument executors, operators and solvers, also covers the MPI
communication layer. By attaching a custom logger to an <code>mpi::communicator</code>, every point-to-point,
collective and synchronization operation issued through that communicator emits a pair of <code>_started</code> and
<code>_completed</code> events.

The example implements a small <code>MpiLogger</code> that derives from <code>gko::log::Logger</code>, subscribes to
<code>gko::log::Logger::mpi_events_mask</code> (which enables all MPI events at once), and prints a per-rank message for
each event. It then exercises the communicator in three ways:
<ol>
  <li> by applying a distributed solver, whose internal halo exchange and norm computations trigger collective
       operations such as <code>all_to_all</code> and <code>all_reduce</code>;
  <li> by computing a distributed dot product, which triggers an <code>all_reduce</code>;
  <li> by issuing explicit blocking and non-blocking point-to-point <code>send</code>/<code>recv</code> calls.
</ol>

For the non-blocking calls, note that the <code>_completed</code> event is reported when <code>request::wait()</code>
returns on the issuing rank, reflecting when the application observes the operation as finished rather than when the MPI
runtime internally completes the transfer.

To run the example with multiple processes, use
`mpirun -n NUM_PROCS ./mpi-logger [executor]`, where the optional executor is `reference` (default) or `omp`.
The example expects at least two processes for the point-to-point section.
